  "text": "Hypothesis 3 is the one the study supports. It matches the abstract on the material (polyacrylamide-TiO2 nanocomposite made by cross-linking), the target (crystal violet removal from water), and the maximum adsorption capacity of 38.9 mg/g. The abstract also concludes the composite is a viable alternative adsorbent.\n\nThe mechanistic part of Hypothesis 3 (electrostatic attraction, hydrogen bonding, n-π interactions) is only partly backed. The abstract reports Monte Carlo and molecular dynamics simulations and a negative adsorption energy, which supports spontaneous CV binding to the surface. It does not name those specific interaction types, so that detail is an inference the abstract doesn't confirm.\n\nThe other hypotheses don't fit. Hypothesis 1 (MWCNT-foam/biochar/bacterial consortium) and Hypothesis 2 (hydrothermally carbonized rice husk with silver nanoparticles) describe different materials. Hypothesis 4 (NaOH-treated wheat straw with electrochemical desorption) also uses a different adsorbent and a two-stage process the study does not describe.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
